check - compare a volume against itself and repair it

  check /home
  check -n /home

FAT records a file's length twice: the directory record holds its size,
and the allocation table holds the chain of clusters. Nothing keeps the
two consistent if power is lost part way through a write. This compares
them and repairs what can be repaired.

Clusters marked used that no file reaches are freed. A chain longer than
its record claims is cut back, and a size larger than its chain is
reduced. A chain that leaves the volume or loops is ended at the last
cluster that was really on it. If the volume has more than one copy of
the allocation table, the copies are brought back into step.

A cluster claimed by two files is reported and not repaired: giving it to
either takes it from the other, and the medium does not record which is
correct. A volume in that state is left mounted read-only.

`-n` reports what is wrong without changing anything.

A volume that was not unmounted cleanly is checked by itself when it is
mounted, so this is only needed to look at one on purpose. Nothing on the
volume may be open while it runs.
